\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Partial Grassmann integration and the Schur complement}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{R,U} be disjoint ordered finite sets. Order the
[independent paired generators](fdip-008F) with all retained
#{R}-pairs first and all eliminated #{U}-pairs second. For
#{n=|R|+|U|}, let
##{K=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\C&D\end{pmatrix}
  \in M_n(\mathbb C).}
Require #{D=K_{UU}} to be invertible and put
#{S=A-BD^{-1}C}. No adjoint relation between #{B,C}
is assumed. Define #{J_U} by taking the coefficient of the
ordered #{U}-pair top word after moving retained generators to
the left. The result is an element of the retained exterior
algebra. Define #{J_R} by its paired top word, so
#{J_RJ_U=J_n}. The eliminated top word has even degree,
so moving it past a retained coefficient gives no further sign. Then
##{J_U\left(e^{-\bar\psi K\psi}\right)
 =\det D\,e^{-\bar\psi_R S\psi_R},}
and consequently
##{\det K=\det D\det S.}
The empty-block conventions are determinant one and the identity
coefficient map. Invertibility of #{K} or #{S} is unnecessary.}

\proof{
\p{Introduce the odd linear combinations
##{\begin{aligned}
\chi_U&=\psi_U+D^{-1}C\psi_R,\\
\bar\chi_U&=\bar\psi_U+\bar\psi_RBD^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
The bars remain labels of independent generators. Expanding in
the displayed multiplication order gives
##{\bar\psi K\psi
 =\bar\psi_R S\psi_R+\bar\chi_U D\chi_U.}
For example, the last term of the expansion of
#{\bar\chi_U D\chi_U} is
#{\bar\psi_RBD^{-1}C\psi_R}, which cancels the subtraction
in #{S}. All these quadratic elements are even. They commute,
so their finite exponentials multiply.}

\p{Replacing an eliminated generator by itself plus a retained
odd linear combination preserves its partial top coefficient.
Indeed, expand each exterior monomial in the new symbols: every
term using a retained replacement has fewer eliminated
generators, while the term using no replacement has coefficient
one and its original order. Terms of lower eliminated degree
cannot contribute to the top degree. Thus the coefficient in
the shifted quadratic exponential equals the coefficient for
independent #{U}-generators. The
[paired determinant identity](fdip-008H) evaluates it as #{\det D}.
The retained exponential factors on the left, proving the
partial formula. Applying #{J_R} and the same determinant
identity proves the full factorization.}
}

\p{The effective matrix is the same block expression as in
[real positive Gaussian elimination](fdip-000D). The scalar here
is #{\det D}; the real Gaussian fiber integral instead has
#{(2\pi)^{|U|/2}(\det D)^{-1/2}} under its real positive
hypotheses and volume convention. Neither positivity nor an
ordinary integration measure was used in the Grassmann proof.}

\p{The finite block identity is also supplied by
\leanref{Matrix.det_fromBlocks₂₂}, for finite index types over a
commutative ring with an invertible eliminated block. This
formal statement supports the determinant factorization, not
the partial Grassmann coefficient operation.}
